        <p class="lead">Between 1949 and 1952, Keith Dunstan filed dispatches for <em>The Australian Home Beautiful</em> from New York, where The Herald and Weekly Times had stationed him as their American correspondent.</p>
        <p>He arrived at twenty-four and was still in his mid-twenties for the whole of this posting — a young reporter in a foreign city, writing for a magazine aimed at the postwar generation of Australian homemakers at the precise moment when American domestic design was beginning to filter through to Australian readers via catalogues and trade shows. His pieces reported on housing trends, design exhibitions and the texture of American domestic life at close quarters.</p>
        <p>The articles held here are transcribed from scans in the National Library of Australia's Trove database.</p>
